# empirical-v3 — the running example's claim-support policy (fig:example)
#
# A policy is a *versioned trusted input* (spec §4, assumption A2). Artifact programs may
# instantiate these schemes but may not define or modify them at runtime.
#
# empirical-v3 is empirical-v1 (the policy the A/B worked examples share) extended with the
# running example's one deductive step: the score-cell vocabulary (`score_cell`, `num_lt`,
# `M0`) and the strict rule `lt_recheck`, certified by the registered ord@1 backend
# against this policy's own (empty) theory. Nothing about the artifact's @0.6 symbolic
# premise-slot spelling reaches this file: a policy declares which certifier a strict rule
# accepts and which theory its certificates cite, and the named-slot spelling is lowered
# away before any certificate is replayed. The version numbering is chronological, not a
# containment chain: empirical-v3 extends v1 alone and does NOT include empirical-v2's
# defense vocabulary (the E4/E5 reinstatement layer).
#
# Every attack in the example is licensed here, not in the artifact program: `undercut` needs an
# `exception` declaration, `rebut`/`undermine` need a `contrary` pair. This is the point of §7 —
# an ARA dead end or a reviewer note only becomes an edge if it can construct one of these typed
# forms against a declaration in THIS file.

policy empirical-v3

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2.
# The base is empirical-v1's Σ (generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` over the shared worked
# examples; see examples/A/empirical-v1.policy.lara), extended by hand with the running
# example's cell vocabulary: `M0` (the baseline system), `score_cell` (a second Report constructor, so the one
# `reports` predicate covers both effect summaries and score cells), and `num_lt`. `Num` and
# `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric and string literals) and are not
# declarable.

sort Experiment, Report, System, Measurand, Dataset, Direction

con D : Dataset
con D_c : Dataset
con D_d : Dataset
con D_j : Dataset
con M : System
con M0 : System
con M_c : System
con M_d : System
con M_j : System
con accuracy : Measurand
con effect(System, Measurand, Dataset, Direction) : Report
con exp_1 : Experiment
con exp_3 : Experiment
con exp_9 : Experiment
con exp_c : Experiment
con exp_cn : Experiment
con exp_cp : Experiment
con exp_d : Experiment
con exp_j : Experiment
con exp_me : Experiment
con exp_ow : Experiment
con kv_quant : System
con latency : Measurand
con mobile_edge : Dataset
con negligible : Direction
con openweb : Dataset
con paperA_exp1 : Experiment
con paperB_exp1 : Experiment
con positive : Direction
con python_code : Dataset
con score_cell(System, Measurand, Dataset, Num) : Report

pred distribution_shift(System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred generalizes(System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred improves(System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred not_generalizes(System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred not_improves(System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred num_lt(Num, Num)
pred powered(Experiment)
pred randomized(Experiment)
pred reports(Experiment, Report)

# --- Inference schemes -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The evidence -> empirical-claim bridge is defeasible (observation O4): a positive experimental
# report presumptively supports an improvement claim, subject to critical questions.
rule controlled_experiment(M, Q, D, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, effect(M, Q, D, positive)) ]
  conclusion = improves(M, Q, D)
  question randomization    : randomized(Exp)        (mandatory)
  question adequate_power   : powered(Exp)           (mandatory)
  question external_validity: generalizes(M, Q, D)   (mandatory)

# A symmetric scheme for a null / negative replication. Concludes the declared contrary of
# `improves`, so an instance of this scheme rebuts an instance of `controlled_experiment` on the
# same (M, Q, D) — this is what lets a *second paper* attack the first (example B).
rule null_result(M, Q, D, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, effect(M, Q, D, negligible)) ]
  conclusion = not_improves(M, Q, D)
  question randomization    : randomized(Exp)        (mandatory)
  question adequate_power   : powered(Exp)           (mandatory)

# --- The certificate-checked ordered comparison ------------------------------------------------
# The running example's one deductive step: an exact ordered re-check of the two reported
# accuracy cells, certified by the registered ord@1 backend (src/Lara/Strict/Ord.hs,
# lean/Lara/Ord.lean). Slot order is the premise list order: slot 0 is the baseline cell,
# slot 1 the system cell. The artifact's certificate cites those slots by the leaf names that
# fill them (lara-syntax@0.6, #105); that spelling is lowered to the numeric slots at
# elaboration, before this rule's certificate is ever replayed.
#
# Both compared values trace to a consulted premise (design §2.4): a threshold or public
# baseline is not a bare goal constant, it enters as a leaf feeding a premise slot.

rule lt_recheck(S, B, Q, D, Exp, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, score_cell(B, Q, D, Bv)),
                 reports(Exp, score_cell(S, Q, D, Sv)) ]
  conclusion = num_lt(Bv, Sv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:empv3-t0) ]

# The empty theory the ord@1 certificates cite: the certified comparison is pure arithmetic, so
# the theory contributes no entries — the digest is selection identity only (the S1 precedent).
theory sha256:empv3-t0 = []

# --- Conflict ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ONLY source of propositional conflict (spec §4, §3.2). Declared in BOTH directions on
# purpose: v0.1 `contrary` is asymmetric (spec §4.1), and `rebut` needs (concl w, concl u) to be
# a declared pair. Mutual rebuttal — two papers each defeating the other into a `contested`
# 2-cycle — therefore requires both orientations. If we later adopt the Path-A involutive
# contradictory map (spec §8.1 flip criterion) this pair collapses to a single `-improves`.
contrary improves(M, Q, D)     not_improves(M, Q, D)
contrary not_improves(M, Q, D) improves(M, Q, D)

# Contrary on the external-validity (generalization) proposition, used by the undermine
# attack in example A: the robustness appendix's `not_generalizes` defeats the leaf that
# discharges a1's external_validity CQ (`generalizes`).
contrary generalizes(M, Q, D)     not_generalizes(M, Q, D)
contrary not_generalizes(M, Q, D) generalizes(M, Q, D)

# --- Exceptions (undercutting conditions) ------------------------------------------------------

# "Shown otherwise", not "not shown": if the eval distribution provably shifts, the
# external-validity of a controlled_experiment instance is defeated (an undercut), not merely
# left open as a gap. Per spec §4.2 a policy chooses, per question, gap-producing (a `question`)
# vs defeat-producing (an `exception`). external_validity appears as BOTH by design.
exception controlled_experiment : distribution_shift(M, Q, D)

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): the only strict rule is lt_recheck, whose conclusion
# pattern num_lt(Bv, Sv) heads no contrary pair, so no contrary side is strict-reachable and
# every proposition in a `contrary` pair remains defeasibly attackable. The policy is therefore
# accepted; a contrary pair touching a strict-reachable proposition would be rejected at compile
# time (R12).
